{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-117951",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's find the missing angle $T.$ Since the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠R+m∠S+m∠T & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {40}^{∘}+{103}^{∘}+m∠T & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠T & ={180}^{∘}-{143}^{∘} \\\\ m∠T & ={37}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∠T$ is the smallest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{RS}$ is the shortest side of the triangle.\n\nSince $∠S$ is the largest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{RT}$ must be the longest side of the triangle. \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nRS<ST<RT\n$$\n\nand therefore, the required order is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{RS},\\overset{―}{ST},\\overset{―}{RT}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
